  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A plume of tropical moisture characterized by \n2\"+ PWATs and dewpoints in the 70s is forecast to get pulled \nnorth through the Southeast U.S. over the weekend to the east of\nthe northward-advancing remnants of TC Isaias. At the surface, \na warm front will trudge north along the Southeast U.S. coast, \npotentially with a weak, non-tropical area of low pressure \ndeveloping along it. Along and ahead of the warm front, a strong\nsoutheasterly LLJ will develop, supporting increasing WAA and \nmoisture transport into the area with a shield of moderate to \nheavy rain developing across the coastal Carolinas. It's \nexpected that this initial swath of precip should support 1-2\" \nof rainfall for much of ENC. Along and just behind the warm \nfront, the precip may become more banded in nature, including \nwith some embedded convective elements. It's these bands that \nwill pose the most intense rainfall rates, as well as the \ngreatest risk of flooding. Convective guidance suggests rainfall\namounts as high as 3-6\"+ are possible if those bands manage to \nreach the coast. As is often the case in these scenarios, it's \nunclear how far inland the instability axis and, consequently, \nthe heaviest rates will get. The higher end rainfall scenario is\nworth mentioning, but it should also be understood that those \namounts are unlikely to be realized if the instability axis \nremains offshore.\n\nAs far as model guidance goes, it's also worth noting that the \nmajority of guidance has trended higher with rainfall totals, \nand the latest forecast amounts reflect this trend. \nAdditionally, various ensemble and forecast aids show a notable \nsignal for heavy rain. A Flood Watch may eventually be needed \nfor a portion of the area if confidence increases regarding the \nhigher end rainfall totals.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Steady increase in moisture through the weekend\npeaks on Sunday contributing to an increase in instability \nacross portions of ENC, greatest south of Highway 264. Factors \nlike cloud cover will determine how much we end up getting, but \nensemble averages suggest CAPEs as high as 1000+ J/kg in some \nspots. While low- level forcing is in question, it's a setup \nthat bears watching for a conditional severe thunderstorm risk. \nMachine learning guidance and EFI guidance have changed little \nfrom yesterday, still suggesting at least a marginal threat. \n\nThe remnants of Isaias are forecast to round the upper level \nridge and move off the Northern Mid-Atlantic Coast early next \nweek. Drier air then moves in behind it with a lower chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms by Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Strengthening onshore winds, building seas, and\nhigher astronomical tides may support some coastal impacts this\nweekend. There is some uncertainty on the magnitude of impacts,\nespecially given the shorter-duration of the stronger winds, as\nwell as what should be a weaker swell compared to the recent\nNor'easter. With that in mind, there will be an increased risk\nof rip currents and large waves, at minimum. With this in mind,\nhave issued a High Surf Advisory for Ocracoke and Hatteras\nIslands, the locations most likely to see 8ft waves in the\nsurfzone from late Saturday night through around sunset on\nSunday. Additionally, some of the most vulnerable locations \nalong the Outer Banks may see at least some minor issues, \nespecially with Sunday morning's high tide (7-9am). There may \nalso be some minor coastal flooding around sounds and tidal \nwaterways around the Crystal Coast due to the higher \nastronomical tides.",
